[2018-11-03T20:55:25Z | Akubi | comment]
@user2684, you are right. 
Arduino is 124 days. 1/3 of year. It's only 62 days if you use long instead of unsigned long.
8 year was from a different implementation (my error).

Concerning the implementation, the problem is that first target will rollover depending on the actual value of millis() and the timer configured _value. So each timer can rollover at a different moment.
When one target value will rollover for a timer, the `if (millis() > _target) return true;` is no more valid.

Maybe a different aproach can be used. If we compute `_target - mills()`, at the begging the result is equal to the Timer configured _value. This value will decrease to zero and then jump to (2^^32) and start decreasing again.
In case of millis() or _target rollover, the result of this operation continue to have the same behavious beacuase the result of operation will also rollover.
So we can now test if the result is > Timer configured _value to detect the Time is over situation.

```
Timer::isOver()
		// check if the current millis() is greater than the target
		if ( (_target - millis()) > _value ) return true;
		return false;
```

Do you agree with this analyses ?

