[2017-01-18T12:30:06Z | yphuangms | comment]
To be specific to the "defect" found in the code (iothubtransport_mqtt_command.c):

-- DoWork() will always try to ensure the connection to be initialized by calling to "InitializeConnect()" . If network is disconnected (socket cannot be opened to connect to target IoT hub), calling to "InitializeConnect()" will fail, and this function will be called again in next "DoWork()" call. The call to "DoWork()" will be managed in a loop in application program.

-- When calls to "InitializeConnect()", if no connection is maintained, this function will try to create the connection ( "SendMqttConnectMsg()" ) again if "CanRetry()" returns true. If the "CanRetry()" always return false, then no connection will ever be established again, even if the network problem is resolved.

-- There's no way to select retry policy in MQTT transportation. In "CreateRetryLogic()", the code shows "retryLogic->fnRetryPolicy = &RetryPolicy_Exponential_BackOff_With_Jitter" for all of retry policy options, even for retry policy == IOTHUB_CLIENT_RETRY_NONE.

-- In "CanRetry()",  there are 3 conditions to return true:

1. 

```
    if (retryLogic == NULL)
    {
        LogError("Retry Logic is not created, retrying forever");
        result = true;
    }

```
But due to a retry policy is set (hardcoded in iothub_client), this condition will never be met.

2. 

```
    if (retryLogic->retryStarted)
    {
        double diffTime = get_difftime(now, retryLogic->lastConnect);
        if (diffTime <= ERROR_TIME_FOR_RETRY_SECS)
        {
            // Just right time to retry
            if(diffTime > WAIT_TIME_SECS)
            {
                retryLogic->lastConnect = now;
                retryLogic->retrycount++;
                result = true;
            }
            else
            {
                // As do_work can be called within as little as 1 ms, wait to avoid throtling server
                result = false;
            }
        }

```

Due to the fact "difftime()" return the time difference in seconds, only when the elapsed time (since last connection retry) is equal to "ERROR_TIME_FOR_RETRY_SECS" (5 seconds), CanRetry will return true. This time window is narrow, and the worst case is, if missing one chance to hit this time window, then the delay time will be again updated (to a even larger number), and the "retryLogic->lastConnect" time is not changed, so the next CanRetry() will get a larger number of elapsed time, so this condition will never be met again before any retry is run. The next chance to run retry is 

            if (retryLogic->fnRetryPolicy != NULL && (retryLogic->fnRetryPolicy(&permit, &delay, retryLogic) == 0))
            {
                if ((permit == true) && ((retryLogic->retryTimeoutLimitInSeconds == 0) || retryLogic->retryTimeoutLimitInSeconds >= (delay + get_difftime(now, retryLogic->start))))
                {
                    retryLogic->delayFromLastConnectToRetry = delay;

                    LogInfo("Evaluated delay %d at %d attempt to retry\n", delay, retryLogic->retrycount);

                    if (retryLogic->delayFromLastConnectToRetry <= ERROR_TIME_FOR_RETRY_SECS)
                    {
                        retryLogic->lastConnect = now;
                        retryLogic->retrycount++;
                        result = true;
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        result = false;
                    }
                }

In this condition, only when the "delay" time returned from "RetryPolicy_Exponential_BackOff_With_Jitter()" is less then or equal to "ERROR_TIME_FOR_RETRY_SECS" (5 seconds), CanRetry() will return true. So, let's do a code trace and see how's the probability this "delay" number will be >= 5, in "RetryPolicy_Exponential_BackOff_With_Jitter()", this delay number will become bigger along with this value "retryLogic->retrycount". This means, as the network error situation continues, this "retryLogic->retrycount" gets bigger, if "DoWork" function is not called at a proper time window, so the program misses one time to retry at the time window of (elapsed time == 5), then it will get a big delay ( >= 5) that will cause the retry never be executed again.

If this "CanRetry()" logic is not changed, then no matter how "ERROR_TIME_FOR_RETRY_SECS" is set, there are always chances the code will fall into a condition that no retry will ever be run. If a retry policy will fall into a hole that no retry will be executed, then this is not an expected "RetryPolicy" should be in a way to common sense. This the reason I report this as a "defect".

Please consider to review the whole retry process, some could be the policy design issue, some could be the coding bug.
Please also consider to enable retry to run in a fixed interval basis (configurable interval).

Thanks!

